WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:01.343
- [Announcer] Touchdown confirmed.

00:00:01.343 --> 00:00:03.010
- [Narrator] On February 18th,

00:00:03.010 --> 00:00:06.633
NASA's Mars 2020 mission
touched down on the red planet.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.210
Since then, its Perseverance rover

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.640
and Ingenuity helicopter have
been exploring the terrain

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.480
and sending back never-before-seen images

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.710
and video of the martian surface.

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:21.905
- The Mars 2020 mission, Mars Perseverance

00:00:21.905 --> 00:00:24.560
has had a string of firsts.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.766
The first sounds from Mars.

00:00:26.766 --> 00:00:28.240
(whooshing sound)

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.680
We can now hear the wind on another world.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:33.200
More practically, it is the first test

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.700
of a new onboard navigation system

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.470
that gives a rover on
Mars greater independence

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:42.870
in how it can guide itself
around potential hazards.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:44.480
- So we haven't just been driving.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.070
We've actually been continuing some

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:47.320
of our tech demos.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:48.390
- [Narrator] Jennifer Trosper

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:51.060
is NASA's Perseverance project manager.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.430
She's been working on the mission

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:53.900
for the past seven years.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.980
- We took on two
technology demonstrations.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.999
One is an instrument called MOXIE

00:00:57.999 --> 00:01:01.270
that actually uses the
carbon dioxide atmosphere

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:02.870
and then produces oxygen.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:04.940
And then, of course, the
other technology demonstration

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:07.210
that we have been successful with

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:10.453
is showing that you can
fly helicopters on Mars.

00:01:12.042 --> 00:01:14.780
- [Narrator] Now after
over 10 months on Mars,

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.810
what have we learned from Perseverance

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:19.570
and Ingenuity that we didn't know before?

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:22.040
And are we any closer to figuring out

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.300
if Mars has what NASA set out to find,

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:27.330
signs of ancient life?

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.820
- The only way to answer
it, NASA believes,

00:01:29.820 --> 00:01:31.430
is to do what they're doing now,

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:35.080
go to another planet,
assess it thoroughly,

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.590
and then attempt to find the chemistry

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:43.060
and fossils that will
prove one way or another

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:46.990
whether there is life of
any sort we can recognize

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:49.000
on a planet other than our own.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.420
- [Narrator] One of the most
important milestones took place

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:54.330
in mid-August when
Perseverance began collecting

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:55.700
rock core samples.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.300
NASA's planning to retrieve

00:01:57.300 --> 00:01:59.430
Perseverance's martian rock samples

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.090
with another spacecraft,

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:04.230
which is expected to
launch from Earth in 2026

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.790
and arrive at Mars in 2028.

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:09.070
- One of the big unanswered questions

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:12.370
that NASA faces in its search for life

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:15.040
and the collection of
these samples on Mars

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:20.010
is can we muster the will

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:23.710
and the funding, and the
engineering expertise

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:26.870
to actually bring these samples home?

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.800
Right now, there are plans
on the drawing board,

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:33.270
there are speeches, there are intentions

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:38.120
but it's a long time
between now and the 2030s

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:41.360
when these missions that
are being projected,

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.250
being talked about to
bring these things home.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.450
- [Narrator] In April,
the Ingenuity helicopter

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.460
became the first craft to fly

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.360
in the atmosphere of another planet.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:52.510
- This technology demonstration

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:55.340
was one for the history books.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:59.450
This helicopter, which was
initially officially designed

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.520
to carry out just five flights

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:03.650
as a technology demonstration

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:06.000
has been flying all over,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.023
it seems sometimes continuously.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:10.330
- [Narrator] The next flights tested

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:13.010
the helicopter's ability
to move horizontally,

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:14.710
and it covered longer distances

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:16.480
in each subsequent flight.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.530
Ingenuity has now flown 18 times.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:20.940
- When we get over to the delta,

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:23.350
Ingenuity can again be used as a scout

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:25.070
to really point Perseverance

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:28.210
to the best places scientifically to go.

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:31.430
And if she's still living,
we have lots of opportunities

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:34.950
for scouting as we move
forward in the mission.

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.840
- [Narrator] NASA selected Jezero Crater

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.770
as Perseverance's landing site

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:40.680
because it gives the rover access

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.250
to a large stack of rocks

00:03:42.250 --> 00:03:45.050
that sits at the end
of a dry river valley.

00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:46.730
Based on satellite images,

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:48.080
scientists have theorized

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.570
that these rocks are made
of sediment deposited

00:03:50.570 --> 00:03:51.840
by an ancient river

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.030
that flowed into a lake
roughly 3.5 billion years ago.

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:58.960
- So Jezero Crater is essentially,

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.570
if you're looking for signs

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.103
of ancient microbial life on Mars,

00:04:02.103 --> 00:04:03.340
this is the place to go.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.140
It's the location, location, location

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:09.770
for finding it because of
that delta that we see there.

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:11.570
- [Narrator] NASA
selected its landing site

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:14.040
because scientists have
long suspected the area

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.610
was once covered in water,

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:17.190
and therefore, had the potential

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.570
for the development of life as we know it.

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:22.490
- But they only could tell this

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:26.400
and target this in the
broadest outlines from orbit.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.950
Now they have boots on the ground

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.865
or wheels if you prefer.

00:04:30.865 --> 00:04:33.560
- [Announcer] (indistinct)
Indicates to deploy.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.040
- [Narrator] Perseverance
landed more than a mile away

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.300
from the cliffs at the front of the delta

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.250
and started taking high-res pictures

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:42.060
to survey the distant rocks.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:45.150
- We basically looked at
the different stratigraphy

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.210
that we saw,

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:48.490
and wanted to confirm that we were right,

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:51.860
that this really is a delta

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:54.390
with a river inflow, and it was deposited,

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:55.870
and there was a lake here.

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:56.890
And based on that,

00:04:56.890 --> 00:04:59.550
we actually were able to confirm that.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:00.780
- [Narrator] On October 7th,

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.040
NASA scientists published
the mission's first study

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.300
in the journal Science.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.678
Based off those images,

00:05:05.678 --> 00:05:08.980
the study found that for
potentially millions of years,

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:11.750
a river flowed into a lake
that filled Jezero Crater.

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:14.260
- We actually have a set of samples

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:18.120
that is very good for
the lower crater area.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.300
We have four core samples.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:23.260
We will have 30 that
we can return to Earth.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:24.460
We'll collect more than that

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:25.972
and then we'll down select.

00:05:25.972 --> 00:05:28.990
And that Earth return
is in the early 2030s.

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:31.010
- [Narrator] In December,
NASA scientists confirmed

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:32.960
yet another long-held suspicion,

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.610
that the martian bedrock
on which Perseverance rover

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.360
has been driving since February

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:39.670
likely formed from red hot magma.

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:42.112
- This was a big surprise to us.

00:05:42.112 --> 00:05:44.799
Certainly not what we expected to find

00:05:44.799 --> 00:05:47.190
that these are in fact volcanic rocks.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:48.120
- [Narrator] This is Perseverance

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:50.070
project scientist Ken Farley

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:53.200
speaking at a recent
conference about the finding.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.310
- This was completely unexpected

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:58.160
and we are struggling to
understand what it means.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:05:59.720
But I will speculate.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:02.370
So this appears to be a product

00:06:02.370 --> 00:06:05.070
that was either intruded into

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:07.130
or erupted into the crater

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:09.511
or flowed in from outside.

00:06:09.511 --> 00:06:12.760
But we don't know exactly
how that would work.

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:14.730
- [Narrator] Although it
landed on the martian surface

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:17.890
in February, Perseverance's
sample return mission

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:20.800
has only really just
started to get under way.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:23.800
- The core samples are the hard evidence,

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.640
and I do mean the hard evidence

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:29.378
of something that we have
been speculating about

00:06:29.378 --> 00:06:31.240
for the longest time.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:35.960
We wanna have some tangible evidence,

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:38.290
something that we can
put under a microscope,

00:06:38.290 --> 00:06:40.810
something that we can
run genetic samples on.

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:43.930
Something that we can
prove beyond a shadow

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:48.930
of a doubt is not the
product of our creation

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:53.376
but is the product of a separate
creation on another world.

00:06:53.376 --> 00:06:56.043
(pensive music)

